    "H"
  ],
  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Look for the product being asked for",
      "body": "<p>Expand the two brackets. Group the terms that contain both variables, and check whether the constant terms cancel.</p>",
      "recap": "An expression in two variables can sometimes depend only on their product.",
      "pitfall": "Neither variable may be zero because both occur in denominators.",
      "pause": "Rewrite the equation using the product of the two variables.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l01",
          "reminder": "Expand carefully, then collect terms before deciding how to solve an equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Name the repeated expression",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Express the equation in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, then multiply through by the nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> to obtain a quadratic.</p>",
      "recap": "A substitution turns the reciprocal expression into an ordinary quadratic.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the nonzero restriction when clearing the denominator.",
      "pause": "Solve the quadratic exactly and retain every permitted real root.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "Choose a replacement variable for the expression that repeats, and record its domain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Return to the requested product",
      "body": "<p>Your roots are possible cubes of the product. Take real cube roots, compare the resulting products, and check that the smaller one can come from nonzero real values of the original variables.</p>",
      "recap": "Solving for a power is only an intermediate step; the requested quantity still has to be recovered.",
      "pitfall": "A real cube root of a negative number is negative.",
      "pause": "Identify the least product and verify it with a valid pair of original variables.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l01",
          "reminder": "Exact surd identities can be verified by raising the proposed expression to the required power."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The denominators require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Expanding the left side gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The equation becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This factors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The real cube-root function is increasing, so the least product comes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, the least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. It is attainable, for example with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer A.</strong></p>",
